{"type":"message","id":"bf913a48","parentId":"bbff4a10","timestamp":"2026-03-08T21:17:51.759Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sun 2026-03-08 22:17 GMT+1] Review the brain-inspired memory implementation roadmap for OpenClaw and identify which features would \"really fit\" the current architecture.\n\nContext: I just completed a deep comparison of human brain memory mechanisms vs OpenClaw's neuro-memory-agent (see memory/2026-03-08-brain-vs-openclaw-comparison.md).\n\nCurrent OpenClaw has:\n- Bayesian surprise detection (90% match to brain)\n- Two-stage retrieval (85% match)\n- Forgetting curves (90% match)\n- Prioritized replay (75% match)\n\nMissing features (ranked by priority):\n1. **Working Memory** - Capacity-limited (~7 items), gates encoding to LTM\n2. **Precision/Attention Weighting** - Modulates surprise by context reliability\n3. **Emotional Valence** - Amygdala-like tagging of significant events\n4. **Semantic Memory Store** - Separate neocortex analogue for facts\n5. **Sleep-Stage Consolidation** - NREM/REM differentiated replay\n6. **Hierarchical Event Segmentation** - Multi-scale temporal boundaries\n\nQuestion: Which of these would **really fit** OpenClaw's current architecture? Consider:\n- Synergy with existing features (event mesh, predictive engine, auto-compaction)\n- Implementation complexity vs impact\n- Architectural compatibility (TypeScript/Python hybrid, MCP servers)\n- Which features would feel \"natural\" vs \"forced\"\n\nRead the full comparison at memory/2026-03-08-brain-vs-openclaw-comparison.md and give specific recommendations with code-level insights."}],"timestamp":1773004671758}}
